<BaseLayout
	title="When the Real Problem Wasn't the Language — Levent Can"
	description="An agent with the lowest NPS on a German-language team was fluent in German. The root cause wasn't comprehension — it was perceived comprehension."
>
	<section class="section prose">
		<a class="back" href="/work">← Work</a>
		<span class="tag">CASE STUDY</span>
		<h1>When the Real Problem Wasn't the Language</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			One agent had the lowest NPS on a German-language team, despite being fluent in German. The
			root cause wasn't comprehension — it was perceived comprehension.
		</p>

		<h3>Problem</h3>
		<p>
			One agent on a German-language account had the lowest NPS on the team and a disproportionate
			share of repeat contacts, dragging FCR down — despite being fluent in German.
		</p>

		<h3>Data</h3>
		<p>NPS and FCR at the individual-agent level, call monitoring notes, team benchmarks.</p>

		<h3>Analysis</h3>
		<p>
			Most agents on German-language accounts are part of the Turkish-German diaspora, born and
			raised in Germany, Austria, or Switzerland — culturally native, not just linguistically
			fluent, which lets them mirror a customer's register and small talk instantly. This
			particular agent had learned German at university: fluent and correct, but with an accent and
			without that reflexive cultural fluency.
		</p>
		<p>
			Listening to the flagged calls showed the issue wasn't comprehension — the agent understood
			every customer correctly. It showed up specifically at the moment the agent had to say no or
			couldn't offer a solution. In those moments, customers seemed to read the accent and slightly
			different conversational rhythm as a sign they hadn't been understood, rather than as a
			genuine, understood "no." That perception — not any actual language or resolution failure —
			was driving the repeat contacts and the low NPS.
		</p>

		<h3>Solution</h3>
		<p>
			Coached the agent to make comprehension explicit instead of implicit: reflect the request
			back before or right after handling it — "So, to make sure I understood correctly, you'd like
			X — is that right?" That one habit gave customers an unambiguous signal they'd been
			understood, independent of accent. Paired with proactively asking for an NPS rating at the end
			of the call — standard, client-sanctioned practice on this account.
		</p>

		<h3>Result</h3>
		<p>The agent's NPS moved from the 60s into the 80s-90s almost immediately.</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

